<h1>Hello from a .hd file</h1><p>This is a body-only HTML fragment — no <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>, or <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> wrappers. Just semantic content.</p><h2>Why this matters</h2><p>Markdown can't easily express things like the table below — merged cells, multi-line content, or inline formatting inside cells. HTML can. The <code>.hd</code> extension signals "documentation HTML fragment" rather than a full HTML page.</p><h2>A table that markdown can't cleanly render</h2><table style="min-width: 495px;"><colgroup><col style="width: 217px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="width: 253px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1" colwidth="217"><p>Feature</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Markdown</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1" colwidth="253"><p>.hd</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" colwidth="217"><p>Basic tables</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Yes</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" colwidth="253"><p>Yes</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" colwidth="217"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" colwidth="253"><p></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" colwidth="217"><p>Merged cells (colspan/rowspan)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>No</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" colwidth="253"><p><strong>Yes</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" colwidth="217"><p>Multi-paragraph cells</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>No</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" colwidth="253"><p><strong>Yes</strong> — like this<br><br>Second paragraph in the same cell.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="2" rowspan="1" colwidth="217,0"><p><em>Merged across two columns →</em></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" colwidth="253"><p>Works fine</p></td></tr></tbody></table><h2>A list</h2><ul><li><p>Headings, paragraphs, lists, links — all standard HTML</p></li><li><p>No <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tags allowed in the document</p></li><li><p>Assets stored alongside the file in a sibling folder (TBD convention)</p></li></ul><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://claude.com/blog/using-claude-code-the-unreasonable-effectiveness-of-html">Reference: Claude blog post on HTML over Markdown</a></p><p><strong>Accessing it from Windows 11:</strong></p><ol><li><p>Open File Explorer.</p></li><li><p>In the address bar type <code>\\</code> followed by the Mac's IP (e.g. <code>\\192.168.1.50</code>) and press Enter.</p></li><li><p>Enter your Mac username and password when prompted (tick "remember" so you don't redo it).</p></li><li><p>The shared folder appears — drag and drop files in, and they copy onto the Mac.</p></li></ol><p>If you want it permanent, right-click the share and "Map network drive" so it shows up as a drive letter every time.</p><img src="image-1.png" alt="image-1.png"><p></p><pre><code>&lt;svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="100%" height="100%" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet" viewBox="0 0 256 256"&gt;&lt;path fill="currentColor" d="M36 128a12 12 0 0 1 12-12h68a12 12 0 0 1 0 24H48a12 12 0 0 1-12-12m12-52h52a12 12 0 0 0 0-24H48a12 12 0 0 0 0 24m132 104H48a12 12 0 0 0 0 24h132a12 12 0 0 0 0-24m52.49-100.49l-40-40a12 12 0 0 0-17 0l-40 40a12 12 0 0 0 17 17L172 77v67a12 12 0 0 0 24 0V77l19.51 19.52a12 12 0 0 0 17-17Z"&gt;&lt;/path&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;</code></pre>